<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

    <!-- ============================================================
         ros2_control HARDWARE DESCRIPTION

         WHAT THIS BLOCK IS FOR, and why it is separate from the
         <link>/<joint> geometry in qarm.urdf.xacro.

         The geometry answers "what shape is the robot". This block
         answers a completely different question: "which joints can
         be commanded, what can be commanded about them, and what
         piece of software talks to the motors".

         Those are independent. The geometry is identical whether
         the arm is simulated or real; only the <plugin> below
         changes. Keeping them in separate files means swapping
         simulation for hardware cannot possibly disturb the
         kinematics, which are validated against the manual to
         1e-16 m and should never be touched by a controls change.

         COMMAND vs STATE INTERFACES - the core concept:

           command_interface   what WE write TO the joint.
                               Here: position. We tell the joint
                               what angle to go to.

           state_interface     what we READ BACK from the joint.
                               Here: position and velocity, i.e.
                               where it actually is and how fast.

         The gap between the two is the entire point of a control
         system. On mock hardware the command is copied straight
         into the state, so the two are always equal and the arm is
         "perfect". On real hardware they differ, and that
         difference is what a controller exists to close.

         WHY position AND NOT effort: the manual offers four write
         modes (Position, PWM, Current, Limp) and flags the last
         three as OPEN LOOP, with the arm needing physical support.
         Position is the only closed-loop mode, so it is the only
         one that maps onto a position command_interface safely.

         WHY THE FINGERS ARE NOT HERE: see the note at the bottom.
         ============================================================ -->

    <xacro:macro name="qarm_ros2_control"
                 params="name:=QArmSystem use_mock_hardware:=true">

        <ros2_control name="${name}" type="system">

            <hardware>
                <xacro:if value="${use_mock_hardware}">
                    <!-- mock_components/GenericSystem is shipped with
                         ros2_control. It is NOT a physics simulator: it
                         copies each position command straight into the
                         matching position state on the next cycle.

                         That sounds useless and is in fact the most
                         valuable debugging tool here, because it makes
                         the hardware layer perfectly predictable. Any
                         misbehaviour seen while running on mock is a bug
                         in the URDF, the controller config or our own
                         node - it cannot be the motors, the wiring or
                         the driver, because none of those are involved.
                         Everything above this line gets debugged with
                         nothing at stake and no risk to the one arm we
                         have. -->
                    <plugin>mock_components/GenericSystem</plugin>

                    <!-- Leave the velocity state at a plain derivative
                         rather than having GenericSystem integrate a
                         dynamics model. We have no inertial data (the
                         manual does not give link masses), so any
                         "dynamics" here would be invented numbers
                         dressed up as simulation. -->
                    <param name="calculate_dynamics">false</param>
                </xacro:if>

                <xacro:unless value="${use_mock_hardware}">
                    <!-- THE REAL DRIVER - not written yet.

                         This is the one line that changes when the
                         hardware interface exists. It will be a C++
                         plugin exporting the same position command and
                         position/velocity state interfaces declared
                         below, so every controller, launch file and node
                         above it keeps working untouched. That
                         substitutability is the whole reason for
                         putting ros2_control underneath our own IK
                         rather than commanding motors directly.

                         Until it exists, launching with
                         use_mock_hardware:=false will fail to load the
                         plugin, loudly and immediately. That is the
                         intended behaviour: better a clear "plugin not
                         found" at startup than a silent fallback to
                         simulation while someone believes they are
                         driving the real arm. -->
                    <plugin>qarm_hardware/QArmSystemHardware</plugin>
                </xacro:unless>
            </hardware>

            <!-- ============================================================
                 THE FOUR ARM JOINTS

                 The command_interface min/max are deliberately the SAME
                 properties the <joint> limits use, not retyped numbers.
                 A joint range lives in exactly one place in this project
                 and everything else derives from it - the same rule that
                 prevented the Day 2 double-counted-kink bug.

                 These limits are a second, independent line of defence.
                 Our IK already filters candidate solutions against the
                 joint ranges, but a command can also arrive from
                 `ros2 topic pub` by hand, or from a future planner with
                 a bug. Declaring the range here means the controller
                 manager refuses an out-of-range command regardless of
                 who sent it.
                 ============================================================ -->

            <joint name="joint_1">
                <command_interface name="position">
                    <param name="min">${q1_min}</param>
                    <param name="max">${q1_max}</param>
                </command_interface>
                <!-- initial_value sets where the arm BELIEVES it is at
                     startup. 0 is the manual's home pose (Figure 4b) for
                     every joint, which is the convention this whole
                     project adopted on Day 3. On mock that makes the arm
                     appear at home. On real hardware this value is
                     ignored - the driver reads the actual encoders, and
                     it must, because the arm is wherever it was left. -->
                <state_interface name="position">
                    <param name="initial_value">0.0</param>
                </state_interface>
                <state_interface name="velocity"/>
            </joint>

            <joint name="joint_2">
                <command_interface name="position">
                    <param name="min">${q2_min}</param>
                    <param name="max">${q2_max}</param>
                </command_interface>
                <state_interface name="position">
                    <param name="initial_value">0.0</param>
                </state_interface>
                <state_interface name="velocity"/>
            </joint>

            <joint name="joint_3">
                <command_interface name="position">
                    <param name="min">${q3_min}</param>
                    <param name="max">${q3_max}</param>
                </command_interface>
                <state_interface name="position">
                    <param name="initial_value">0.0</param>
                </state_interface>
                <state_interface name="velocity"/>
            </joint>

            <joint name="joint_4">
                <command_interface name="position">
                    <param name="min">${q4_min}</param>
                    <param name="max">${q4_max}</param>
                </command_interface>
                <state_interface name="position">
                    <param name="initial_value">0.0</param>
                </state_interface>
                <state_interface name="velocity"/>
            </joint>

            <!-- ============================================================
                 THE FINGERS ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT.

                 finger_left_joint and its mimicking finger_right_joint
                 stay outside ros2_control for now, and the existing
                 qarm_gripper node keeps publishing them on
                 /joint_states exactly as it does today.

                 WHY: robot_state_publisher merges PARTIAL joint states
                 from several publishers, so joint_state_broadcaster can
                 own the four arm joints while qarm_gripper owns the two
                 finger joints, with neither one overwriting the other.
                 That is the same two-publisher split that already works
                 today, and it means bringing up ros2_control does not
                 break a feature that is finished and tested.

                 WHY NOT PERMANENTLY: on real hardware the gripper has
                 to be driven through the hardware interface like
                 everything else, so the fingers will have to move in
                 here eventually, behind a gripper controller. Doing it
                 now would mean rewriting a working node in the same
                 change that introduces ros2_control, and debugging two
                 new things at once. Tracked as an open item.
                 ============================================================ -->

        </ros2_control>

    </xacro:macro>

</robot>
